WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:23.840
DAVID IGNATIUS: If those of you who are still
milling about -- if you could take your seats.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.590
There's a lot of interest in this tonight,
this subject.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.870
We want to get started right off.

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:39.980
We were saying before it's a little intimidating
for us up here on the stage to look out on

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:44.040
you and see -- can everyone hear me OK?

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.040
&gt;&gt;MR.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:46.040
: Yeah.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.040
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: No?

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.040
&gt;&gt;MR.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.040
: No, I don't think you're on yet.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.950
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: We were assured we weren't going
to have to turn these on -- you can hear me

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:54.950
now, right?

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:55.950
Thanks.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:01:01.470
We were just saying before we came up here
that it's a little unnerving to look out at

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:06.220
you and see so many well-informed people on
South Asia issues.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:10.490
It's kind of -- kind of raises the expectations
on us up here on the stage.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:12.280
We hope we can do our best.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:15.640
So welcome to today's Council on Foreign Relations
meeting.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:22.840
I have to begin by asking, as we always do,
for you to turn off your cellphones -- turn

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.950
them off, not just put them on vibrate.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:29.890
As someone who works in radio, I can tell
you that even on vibrate, they can cause electronic

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:31.560
interference with the sound system.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.770
So if you would please turn off all your wireless
devices.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:44.490
One other point, as a reminder: This meeting,
unlike some council meetings, is on the record.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:49.360
So that's something for us to keep in mind
as well as you in the audience.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:56.750
One other announcement: the council is pleased
to announce an upcoming lunch meeting next

00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:03.130
-- not next Tuesday -- Tuesday, February 14th:
The U.S. Aging Population as an Economic Growth

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:06.090
Driver for Global Competitiveness.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:11.960
And the guests for that event are going to
be Joseph Coughlin (sp), Robert Hormats and

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:13.980
Kelly Michael (sp).

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:19.510
For more information on that and other upcoming
events, please refer to the insert in the

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:22.430
back of tonight's program.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:27.040
So the topic again tonight is U.S.-Pakistan
relations.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.680
And we really do have an all-star panel.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:34.480
Let me just begin with, to my far right, Steve
Coll.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.160
I'm sure you all know him, president of the
New America Foundation and staff writer for

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:46.640
the New Yorker magazine, before that, a distinguished
foreign correspondent, the author of two -- not

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.120
one, two Pulitzer Prize-winning books.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:55.430
And the one that is of most relevance tonight
is of course "Ghost Wars: The Secret History

00:02:55.430 --> 00:03:03.030
of the CIA, Afghanistan and bin Laden from
the Soviet Invasion to September 10th, 2001."

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:07.780
In the middle, Bob Grenier, currently chairman
of EIG Partners.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:15.040
But for our purposes tonight, he'll be speaking
on the basis of his 27 years of experience

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.700
in the intelligence community.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:23.160
A veteran of the CIA, Bob most recently served
as director of the CIA's Counterterrorism

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.490
Center.

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:31.450
Prior to that, he was Iraq mission manager,
and prior to that, the agency's station chief

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:40.280
in Islamabad from 1999 to 2002, in other words,
spanning the 9/11 attacks in that post.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:45.840
And on my immediate right, Dan Markey, senior
fellow for India, Pakistan and South Asia

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:47.090
here at the council.

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:52.060
And here he specializes in security and governance
issues in South Asia.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:56.069
He's currently writing a book -- good luck
with that, Dan -- on the future of the U.S.-Pakistan

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:57.330
relationship.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:02.909
Prior to that, he was -- he handled the South
Asia portfolio in the policy planning staff

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:04.770
at the State Department.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:10.060
And of course, he was project director of
the council- sponsored Independent Task Force

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:15.110
on U.S. strategy in Pakistan and Afghanistan,
and some of the recommendations of that task

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:19.609
force of course will be the subject of our
discussion tonight.

00:04:19.609 --> 00:04:26.599
So tonight we are focusing on one of the most
complex and problematic situations in the

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:34.169
world today: the multifaceted and multilateral
relationship between Pakistan, the United

00:04:34.169 --> 00:04:38.139
States, Afghanistan and the Taliban.

00:04:38.139 --> 00:04:43.979
I would say that this is a critical moment
in U.S.-Pakistan relations, but we have said

00:04:43.979 --> 00:04:47.840
that so many times before that it's become
a cliche.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:54.699
So rather than make that rather lame observation,
let me just roll off a few of the items that

00:04:54.699 --> 00:04:57.150
really set the stage for this discussion tonight.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:01.680
Going back just to last May and of course
the raid that resulted in the killing of Osama

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:07.099
bin Laden -- and we've since learned that
a Pakistani doctor who provided some of the

00:05:07.099 --> 00:05:14.809
key intelligence that made that raid possible
has been arrested and is facing possible treason

00:05:14.809 --> 00:05:23.559
charges, just one indication of the rage that
that raid engendered in Pakistan among those

00:05:23.559 --> 00:05:30.629
who feel that their sovereignty was violated;
and of course the United States, in September,

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:35.259
the chairman of the Joint Chiefs, Admiral
Mullen, very publicly accusing Pakistan's

00:05:35.259 --> 00:05:40.710
intelligence service of supporting the Haqqani
terrorist network, which has been the main

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:46.110
enemy of U.S. forces in Afghanistan, and then
subsequent to that, just two months later,

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:52.889
the U.S. air and helicopter strikes that resulted
in the death of 24 Pakistani troops along

00:05:52.889 --> 00:05:58.259
the border when they were caught in the middle
of that and, of course, the Pakistanis again,

00:05:58.259 --> 00:06:05.849
in their rage at that violation of their sovereignty,
in their view, ordered an end to U.S. supply

00:06:05.849 --> 00:06:10.659
and resupply operations through Pakistan for
operations in Afghanistan; and then, of course,

00:06:10.659 --> 00:06:17.120
finally and most recently, the controversy
over the memo allegedly written by Pakistan's

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:23.229
ambassador to the United States, Husain Haqqani,
in which he is said to have asked for U.S.

00:06:23.229 --> 00:06:28.940
support to guard against a possible military
coup in Afghanistan (sic) on the part of the

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:34.759
Pakistani army and of course that then provoked
one of the most serious civil- military conflicts

00:06:34.759 --> 00:06:37.889
in Pakistan in many years.

00:06:37.889 --> 00:06:42.860
So that's not even to mention the broader
context in which all these things are occurring

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:49.839
-- the prospect of a U.S. withdrawal from
Afghanistan in less than two years, and the

00:06:49.839 --> 00:06:57.330
preparations that are under way for that including
the possible arrangement of negotiations with

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:04.929
the Taliban in Qatar -- so those are the -- that's
sort of the parameters of the situation that

00:07:04.929 --> 00:07:06.610
we're going to be talking about tonight.

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:12.270
Dan, I want to start with you just to give
us like a 30-second summary of where you see

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:15.639
U.S.-Pakistan relations right at this moment.

00:07:15.639 --> 00:07:20.879
Where do things stand in terms of, let's say,
the aid, the cutoff of aid, the refusal by

00:07:20.879 --> 00:07:24.339
Pakistan to accept aid, the supply line issue?

00:07:24.339 --> 00:07:27.179
Sort of, in a -- in a nutshell, where do things
stand right now?

00:07:27.179 --> 00:07:32.879
&gt;&gt;DANIEL MARKEY: Well, in a nutshell, we are
currently at a place which is certainly worse

00:07:32.879 --> 00:07:37.789
in the bilateral relationship than we've had
since 9/11, there's no doubt.

00:07:37.789 --> 00:07:41.819
So, yes, we've seen many crises over the past
few years.

00:07:41.819 --> 00:07:46.619
I was just thinking today is my fifth anniversary
here at the council and, as soon as I showed

00:07:46.619 --> 00:07:48.119
up, things went haywire.

00:07:48.119 --> 00:07:49.619
No connection, of course --

00:07:49.619 --> 00:07:50.619
&gt;&gt;MR.

00:07:50.619 --> 00:07:51.619
: (Inaudible) -- your area.

00:07:51.619 --> 00:07:52.629
&gt;&gt;MARKEY: Yeah, no connection.

00:07:52.629 --> 00:07:55.219
But we've seen crisis after crisis.

00:07:55.219 --> 00:08:00.059
But, over the past 18 months, it's been a
step-wise series of events, each one knocking

00:08:00.059 --> 00:08:06.819
us down from where we were to a point which
is as low as we've seen since 9/11.

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:12.309
Now, in terms of our supply routes into Afghanistan
and so on, I think some of these are sort

00:08:12.309 --> 00:08:14.800
of transitory issues.

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:20.029
It sounds like at least some of that will
be reopened in the -- in the next few weeks.

00:08:20.029 --> 00:08:23.569
But there's a broader question here and it's
a question that's been certainly on Pakistani

00:08:23.569 --> 00:08:29.349
minds over the past two years and, in some
ways, much longer than that, which is how

00:08:29.349 --> 00:08:33.539
we intend to resolve this end game in Afghanistan.

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:40.219
And I think, in many ways, that's the context
that they see us in the region.

00:08:40.219 --> 00:08:41.649
That's how they perceive us.

00:08:41.649 --> 00:08:45.709
And the question in their mind is, how are
we going to leave things?

00:08:45.709 --> 00:08:50.300
Are we going to leave Afghanistan the sort
of mess that they -- that we felt we left

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:55.860
it at the end of the Soviet occupation?

00:08:55.860 --> 00:09:01.430
Are we going to make it even worse for them,
in a sense, by doing things that might say

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:06.709
privilege an Indian involvement in Afghanistan?

00:09:06.709 --> 00:09:13.569
And will the way that we leave it leave us
with a really broken relationship between

00:09:13.569 --> 00:09:16.110
Washington and Islamabad?

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:19.040
I think that's the framework.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:22.579
When you boil it down, they're -- each one
of these incidents that we've seen over the

00:09:22.579 --> 00:09:29.230
past year in some ways relates to that and
the deep uncertainty on the part of the Pakistanis

00:09:29.230 --> 00:09:36.199
about what our intentions are as we prepare
for this departure that you mentioned in Afghanistan.

00:09:36.199 --> 00:09:40.339
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Well, and that would be coming
up in a couple of years and that's where the

00:09:40.339 --> 00:09:42.610
United States is positioned right now.

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:48.769
You know, something that all three of you
have observed is in reference to Pakistan's

00:09:48.769 --> 00:09:54.380
support for the Taliban or for the Haqqani
network -- all three of you have been able

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:59.009
to -- have focused on sort of the rationality
of that position.

00:09:59.009 --> 00:10:03.930
And Bob, I wanted -- I was struck by something
that you write in sort of the classic analysis

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:10.540
of an intelligence professional, talking about
Pakistan's support for the Taliban: "Given

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:15.339
their perception of their national interests
and the lack of effective alternative methods

00:10:15.339 --> 00:10:19.939
to pursue them, one can readily see why the
Pakistanis behave as they do.

00:10:19.939 --> 00:10:23.620
I may disapprove as an American, but as a
political realist, I cannot fault them."

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:25.990
Do you want to elaborate on that a little
bit?

00:10:25.990 --> 00:10:29.670
&gt;&gt;ROBERT GRENIER: That's the problem with
writing.

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:32.769
Somebody's likely to read it at some point.

00:10:32.769 --> 00:10:33.769
(Laughter.)

00:10:33.769 --> 00:10:41.250
Yeah, you know, the -- one of the real problems,
I think, that we all have in dealing with

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:47.269
Pakistan is it's very difficult to get past
one's emotions.The Pakistanis are maddening

00:10:47.269 --> 00:10:49.850
as so-called allies.

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:56.259
They -- often, what they do, even when it's
in their interests, is, frankly, rather ignoble.

00:10:56.259 --> 00:10:59.339
Even when we understand it, we certainly can't
approve of it.

00:10:59.339 --> 00:11:04.009
And so I think it's a tremendous burden, I
think, for policymakers in dealing with those

00:11:04.009 --> 00:11:05.009
issues in the first place.

00:11:05.009 --> 00:11:09.000
And then, trying to sell them to a political
public is almost impossible.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:16.040
But, yeah, I think that for just the reasons
that Dan has just mentioned, at this point,

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:21.320
the U.S. has not decided for itself, I think,
first of all -- nor, clearly, in conjunction

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:24.980
with the Afghan government -- what our posture
is going to look like in Afghanistan; how

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:30.370
are we going to perceive our interests in
the future and how are we going to try to

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:31.569
realize them.

00:11:31.569 --> 00:11:35.899
And under those circumstances, it becomes
very, very difficult for the Pakistanis to

00:11:35.899 --> 00:11:38.379
imagine what that's going to look like.

00:11:38.379 --> 00:11:42.899
And I'm sure that U.S. policymakers are telling
them to some degree what that's going to look

00:11:42.899 --> 00:11:45.300
like, but we have very, very little credibility.

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:51.470
And it seems to me that unless and until we
make very clear what will be the limits, if

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:56.610
you will, of Pakistani aspirations in Afghanistan,
we're not going to be able to get to anything

00:11:56.610 --> 00:12:03.100
approaching an agreement with the Pakistanis
as to what -- how they will pursue their interests

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:11.829
and how we can work out some sort of a rough
-- a rough joint approach in coming up with

00:12:11.829 --> 00:12:14.990
something which at the end of the day will
serve our interests and theirs.

00:12:14.990 --> 00:12:18.160
The way things are right now, they assume,
as Dan has said, that we're going to be leaving

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:23.149
very shortly, and the only real ally that
they have, potential ally, is the Taliban,

00:12:23.149 --> 00:12:25.910
and they're certainly not going to cut ties
with the Taliban under those circumstances.

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:30.279
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Do you think the Pakistanis actually
know what their -- what the limits of their

00:12:30.279 --> 00:12:31.639
activity in Afghanistan should be?

00:12:31.639 --> 00:12:35.470
And are they capable of confining themselves
to those limits?

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:40.120
&gt;&gt;GRENIER: Well, I don't think that the problem
is with the Pakistanis being able to confine

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:41.120
themselves.

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:45.569
I think that the problem with the Pakistanis
is getting them to do anything at all, even

00:12:45.569 --> 00:12:48.140
in pursuit of their own interests.

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:52.499
People tend to think of -- I mean, we see
written all the time that the Taliban is in

00:12:52.499 --> 00:12:58.649
thrall to the Pakistanis, that the leadership
of the Taliban is essentially under their

00:12:58.649 --> 00:12:59.649
control.

00:12:59.649 --> 00:13:01.540
I don't think that that could be further from
the truth.

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:05.199
I think they have a very complicated relation
with -- relationship with them.

00:13:05.199 --> 00:13:07.060
I think they try to influence them in variousways.

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:08.410
They certainly don't control them.

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:12.350
And I don't think they've figured out for
themselves, quite frankly, as to what are

00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:14.949
achievable national goals in Afghanistan.

00:13:14.949 --> 00:13:21.889
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Steve, I read with great interest
the New York Times story today summarizing

00:13:21.889 --> 00:13:31.140
this recent classified report on the state
of the Taliban 2012 that's based on 27,000

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:33.870
interrogations of 4,000 Taliban insurgents.

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:36.930
And I think for me, and I think probably for
a lot of people, one of the lines in that

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:45.019
report that really jumped out was an observation
that many Afghans are already bracing themselves

00:13:45.019 --> 00:13:50.069
for the prospect of a Taliban takeover in
Afghanistan.

00:13:50.069 --> 00:13:54.360
To what extent would you say that the Pakistanis
are also assuming that?

00:13:54.360 --> 00:14:00.139
And again, does that explain their determination
to sort of maintain influence with the Taliban,

00:14:00.139 --> 00:14:04.070
because they see themselves as dealing with
the future power in Afghanistan?

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:10.810
&gt;&gt;STEVE COLL: Well, I think it's important
to recognize that their timelines are quite

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:11.990
different than our own.

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:13.350
I mean, they're in the neighborhood forever.

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:17.860
We have a plan that we've announced that has
very specific calendar dates that relate to

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:22.999
our political situation at home and in Europe,
and our fiscal sustainability problems.

00:14:22.999 --> 00:14:28.000
And they're not thinking about our timeline;
they're thinking about their own, for obvious

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:29.000
reasons.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:35.999
And my sense of the best guess -- because
nobody has a crystal ball about Afghanistan

00:14:35.999 --> 00:14:44.370
-- that the Pakistani high command, core commanders,
are operating from, is that they're doubtful

00:14:44.370 --> 00:14:48.040
that the Pakistani army that -- I mean the
Afghan army that we're -- that we're building

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:54.959
will hold together beyond 2016 or 2018.

00:14:54.959 --> 00:14:57.439
They see trouble on the horizon after we go.

00:14:57.439 --> 00:15:04.769
And I think part of the way to understand
their caution and passivity now is this thinking

00:15:04.769 --> 00:15:06.730
about a longer horizon.

00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:12.620
Now, I think the second point I'd make about
that is that I think the Pakistani Army is

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:14.540
genuinely in a pretty weak position.

00:15:14.540 --> 00:15:18.910
They've got trouble on the horizon, and they
don't have a lot of cards to play.

00:15:18.910 --> 00:15:22.910
We're not their partners in securing their
interests in an Afghan transition, they believe

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:27.939
-- at least we haven't established ourselves,
in their minds, as reliable partners.

00:15:27.939 --> 00:15:35.189
They see India, Iran and other interests -- other
neighbors who don't have, in their mind, Pakistan's

00:15:35.189 --> 00:15:39.750
best interests at heart, already preparing
for a post-NATO Afghanistan.

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:44.189
And, you know, the Taliban, the Quetta shura
leadership that -- the old Islamic Emirate

00:15:44.189 --> 00:15:52.050
leadership that's in Pakistan, and the Haqqanis,
as distasteful and difficult and troublesome

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:56.499
as they are as clients -- and they are difficult
clients, who would like, themselves, to be

00:15:56.499 --> 00:16:02.069
free of Pakistani influence and coercion -- are
about the only option that they -- that they've

00:16:02.069 --> 00:16:03.069
got.

00:16:03.069 --> 00:16:09.420
And so caution and a -- and a longer timeline
makes all the sense in that respect.

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:14.329
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Now, you just had a terrific article
in the New Yorker on Mullah Omar.

00:16:14.329 --> 00:16:18.649
And one of the things that I thought was most
admirable about it was the nuance in your

00:16:18.649 --> 00:16:19.649
analysis.

00:16:19.649 --> 00:16:24.129
I mean, you know, it's so easy for us to just
sort of assume that the Taliban are somehow

00:16:24.129 --> 00:16:27.360
operating as puppets of the ISI.

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:31.119
You pointed out that -- actually, that there's
a lot of strains in that relationship.

00:16:31.119 --> 00:16:37.509
&gt;&gt;COLL: Yeah, well, certainly the Times report
that you referred to and the document itself,

00:16:37.509 --> 00:16:42.839
excerpts of which have now been released,
make clear how the Taliban themselves see

00:16:42.839 --> 00:16:44.589
their relationship with Pakistan.

00:16:44.589 --> 00:16:51.220
And they are -- Taliban leaders and middle-level
commanders are -- they're Afghans, first of

00:16:51.220 --> 00:16:52.220
all.

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:55.120
They wish to operate independently.

00:16:55.120 --> 00:17:01.790
They resent the extent of dependency that
they sometimes have on the Pakistani state

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:07.059
for travel documents, for security, for the
security of their family members.

00:17:07.059 --> 00:17:12.160
If you're living in exile in Pakistan, and
the Pakistani state knows you're there, and

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:15.529
you're a person of significance in the Taliban
command, you're really not a free -- you're

00:17:15.529 --> 00:17:16.549
really not a free man.

00:17:16.549 --> 00:17:21.630
You may not be under ISI's control, but you
are certainly under their influence, and you're

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:25.530
dependent upon them in logistical ways.

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:29.340
And another point that Afghans have often
made to me -- and I -- when I was in Kandahar

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:32.940
this last time in December, I heard it again
and again -- you know, from the Taliban's

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:40.800
perspective, if you're trying to even consider
conversion from revolutionary violence into

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:48.000
political negotiation, it is not in your interest
-- it's not viable to enter those negotiations

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:53.090
while being seen by other Afghans as an instrument
of the Pakistani state because that weakens

00:17:53.090 --> 00:17:57.049
your own credibility with your countrymen.

00:17:57.049 --> 00:18:03.570
The Afghans who are in the Taliban leadership
who are exploring negotiations, I think, would

00:18:03.570 --> 00:18:09.360
like to enter those negotiations from the
most independent posture possible.

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:17.700
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Bob and Dan, Steve mentioned the
sort of weakness of the -- of the Pakistani

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:23.730
army, which may strike some as kind of an
odd observation given the role that the Pakistani

00:18:23.730 --> 00:18:27.429
army has played in Pakistani politics for
so many years.

00:18:27.429 --> 00:18:34.850
But I'm curious what your view is of the position
of the Pakistani army right now vis-a-vis

00:18:34.850 --> 00:18:39.549
other institutions in Pakistan and in the
midst of this crisis.

00:18:39.549 --> 00:18:44.680
Now, Bob, you wrote that they are, in some
ways, the most competent institution in Pakistan.

00:18:44.680 --> 00:18:49.890
And yet you don't seem to thinkthat they're
really in much of a position to exert that

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:51.899
competence right now, it seems.

00:18:51.899 --> 00:18:58.919
&gt;&gt;GRENIER: I think that if you view the situation
from the perspective of the senior Pakistani

00:18:58.919 --> 00:19:03.740
officer corps, they're in a very difficult
situation.

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:08.740
They are facing multiple insurgencies within
their own country, which they're having a

00:19:08.740 --> 00:19:11.029
very difficult time managing.

00:19:11.029 --> 00:19:20.559
They are very concerned lest the Afghan-focused
militants in the area combine efforts with

00:19:20.559 --> 00:19:23.370
the Pakistan-focused militants against them.

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:27.510
And that's something that they absolutely
have to avoid and is one of the root causes

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:32.149
for some of what we would regard as their
very unfortunate behavior vis-a-vis the Afghan

00:19:32.149 --> 00:19:34.750
Taliban.

00:19:34.750 --> 00:19:41.240
They have lost a great deal of credibility,
particularly -- well, since the reign of General

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:45.159
Musharraf, and now most particularly since
the raid at Abbottabad.

00:19:45.159 --> 00:19:48.850
They don't have the domestic political support
to step in and take over the government if

00:19:48.850 --> 00:19:51.520
they felt the need to do so.

00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:56.380
And so they are having to make accommodations
all the way around, whether it's to the Americans,

00:19:56.380 --> 00:20:00.170
the Afghan Taliban, to their own militants
and to a civilian government, which they don't

00:20:00.170 --> 00:20:01.490
like and don't trust.

00:20:01.490 --> 00:20:03.760
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: And Dan, what does this mean for
the United States?

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:07.549
You know, Musharraf was a very important ally
of U.S. administrations.

00:20:07.549 --> 00:20:12.690
You know, does the U.S. have a dog in this
fight?

00:20:12.690 --> 00:20:17.580
I mean, what is -- what is the outcome sort
of with -- internally in Pakistan that would

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:20.120
be most favorable to U.S. strategic interests?

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:26.790
&gt;&gt;MARKEY: Well, it's a -- it's a really different
and unusual time in Pakistani history insofar

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:32.760
as, as Bob pointed out, the military doesn't
have the legitimacy, the credibility, the

00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:38.029
strength, the political punch that it had
had in previous generations.

00:20:38.029 --> 00:20:45.419
And I remember reading not long ago an interesting
Pakistani column that pointed out that not

00:20:45.419 --> 00:20:49.930
long ago you would have expected, given the
range of crisis that we've seen over the past

00:20:49.930 --> 00:20:58.410
year, that at any given time you would have
seen a Pakistani, probably General Kayani,

00:20:58.410 --> 00:21:03.940
come onto the television sometime in the evening
and announce that he had been forced, for

00:21:03.940 --> 00:21:08.250
national security reasons, to assert the army's
control over the state.

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:10.190
And yet you haven't seen that.

00:21:10.190 --> 00:21:15.640
And you've seen the rise -- and Bob alluded
to this -- of other independent power centers

00:21:15.640 --> 00:21:18.159
in Pakistani politics.

00:21:18.159 --> 00:21:23.270
It's partially the fact that the army was
tarnished by the years of Musharraf's rule.

00:21:23.270 --> 00:21:28.110
It's partially that it was tarnished even
by recent events by being too connected to

00:21:28.110 --> 00:21:33.680
the United States, by looking weak, by looking
weak after the bin Laden raid and so on.

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:39.360
It's partially those things, but it's also
the rise of the -- of a more independent media,

00:21:39.360 --> 00:21:45.929
of political opposition, politicians and so
on, and by the judiciary, which also has asserted

00:21:45.929 --> 00:21:49.279
itself in ways that we'd never seen before
in Pakistani history.

00:21:49.279 --> 00:21:55.059
So they do feel -- the army does feel somewhat
more constrained than it has in the past.

00:21:55.059 --> 00:21:59.510
And yet, if you peel away some of these pieces,
you see that they're still pulling a lot of

00:21:59.510 --> 00:22:05.970
the strings from behind the scenes, particularly
with respect to national security strategy

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:07.130
and to regional strategy.

00:22:07.130 --> 00:22:11.100
There's no one else who's really running that
policy other than the army.

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:16.110
So while they feel very constrained and hemmed
in in ways that they haven't before, they

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:20.649
don't have that free hand, and it also makes
it somewhat more complicated to figure out

00:22:20.649 --> 00:22:26.770
who's really in charge, because it's not transparent,
yet there's no one else -- the president,

00:22:26.770 --> 00:22:31.919
the prime minister, the parliament as a whole,
the public or some other -- you know, you

00:22:31.919 --> 00:22:34.789
can't find somewhere else where you would
find that power center.

00:22:34.789 --> 00:22:40.110
So it does complicate things for us in a -- in
a diplomatic sense.

00:22:40.110 --> 00:22:44.059
And it also -- I think it leads to a certain
amount of stalemate and gridlock in their

00:22:44.059 --> 00:22:50.000
own system, and it's not surprising that they
seem sometimes sort of befuddled and caught

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:55.940
unaware by events as they happen and that
we get somewhat contradictory or confusing

00:22:55.940 --> 00:22:56.940
outcomes.

00:22:56.940 --> 00:23:01.390
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Now, another cliche in talking
about Pakistan is that they view India as

00:23:01.390 --> 00:23:07.820
their existential enemy, and you have to sort
of keep that in mind always in analyzing Pakistani

00:23:07.820 --> 00:23:08.820
behavior.

00:23:08.820 --> 00:23:15.159
Sort of bring up to date on that relationship
and that focus, because, you know, one of

00:23:15.159 --> 00:23:20.250
the developments in the last few months that
hasn't gotten a tremendous amount of attention

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:27.510
is the sort of very tentative rapprochement
-- that might even be too strong a word -- between

00:23:27.510 --> 00:23:31.880
India and Pakistan; some confidence-building
measures, I think, is probably the best to

00:23:31.880 --> 00:23:32.880
categorize it.

00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:34.230
And what's the significance of that?

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:40.210
And has this changed at all the strategic
outlook of the Pakistani military and security

00:23:40.210 --> 00:23:41.210
forces?

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:44.070
&gt;&gt;MARKEY: And it's interesting; you have seen
this surprising mellowing of the relationship

00:23:44.070 --> 00:23:51.179
between, in a sense -- between Islamabad and
New Delhi -- certainly unanticipated in New

00:23:51.179 --> 00:23:53.350
Delhi, as far as I can tell.

00:23:53.350 --> 00:23:59.570
I think Indians were somewhat caught by surprise
by some of the overtures that the Pakistanis

00:23:59.570 --> 00:24:03.399
have made in recent months, including on the
trade front.

00:24:03.399 --> 00:24:07.299
The Indians that I spoke with were sort of,
where did this come from?

00:24:07.299 --> 00:24:09.039
What's driving this?

00:24:09.039 --> 00:24:12.110
And I don't really have an answer, except
for two observations.

00:24:12.110 --> 00:24:18.080
One would be that on the part -- on the part
of the Pakistani civilian politicians, there

00:24:18.080 --> 00:24:23.149
has been an openness and an eagerness to engage,
particularly on the economic front, with India

00:24:23.149 --> 00:24:24.280
for quite some time.

00:24:24.280 --> 00:24:29.320
And if given the opportunity, they have been
inclined to seize that opportunity.

00:24:29.320 --> 00:24:34.840
So they've moved forward or tried to move
forward on most favored nation status agreements,

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:40.990
to open up a variety of -- trade in a variety
of sectors that had been closed.

00:24:40.990 --> 00:24:45.750
At the same time, it appears that the army
may have been looking, as it felt hemmed in

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:52.440
on all of these other fronts, to try to at
least ease the pressure on the Indian front

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:57.870
and therefore open the door a bit to the Indian
politicians -- sorry, to the -- to the Pakistani

00:24:57.870 --> 00:25:00.450
politicians to make that overture to India.

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:02.179
But it has only gone so far.

00:25:02.179 --> 00:25:07.280
It's not -- this is -- there's nothing that
is irreversible about this.

00:25:07.280 --> 00:25:11.260
There's nothing that's really been accomplished
that is concrete.

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:15.700
But it's certainly positive and it's certainly
a welcome change.

00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:22.830
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Steve, with all of this as background
-- you know, the situation between Afghanistan,

00:25:22.830 --> 00:25:29.320
the Taliban, Pakistan, the United States,
India -- what's your sense of Pakistan's view

00:25:29.320 --> 00:25:35.799
of the prospect of negotiations between the
United States and Taliban -- initially kind

00:25:35.799 --> 00:25:38.220
of a two-sided negotiation?

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:43.480
It's kind of strange that Pakistan doesn't
seem to be as much of a party to that process

00:25:43.480 --> 00:25:45.340
as you might think they should be.

00:25:45.340 --> 00:25:48.240
What's your sense of what their view of it
is and their interest in it?

00:25:48.240 --> 00:25:50.970
&gt;&gt;COLL: Well, I think they have an interest
in it.

00:25:50.970 --> 00:25:57.330
I think they do feel aggrieved about the extent
to which they've been involved.

00:25:57.330 --> 00:26:00.770
Whether their grievances are justified or
not is another matter; they feel aggrieved

00:26:00.770 --> 00:26:04.440
about quite a lot of things about the U.S.
relationship.

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:10.340
The Afghan government and other parties to
the negotiations, I think probably including

00:26:10.340 --> 00:26:15.900
sections of our government, are not persuaded
that putting the Pakistanis in the lead in

00:26:15.900 --> 00:26:19.130
this negotiation is a really wise way to get
it started.

00:26:19.130 --> 00:26:22.990
So there's been tension about the extent to
which the United States has been briefing

00:26:22.990 --> 00:26:25.440
the Pakistanis.

00:26:25.440 --> 00:26:30.120
That yielded, I think, over the last six months
or so to a much franker exchange between both

00:26:30.120 --> 00:26:34.350
the Pakistanis and the Americans about where
this thing was going, what it was going to

00:26:34.350 --> 00:26:35.419
look like.

00:26:35.419 --> 00:26:41.279
But I -- my sense is that the Pakistanis have
been reluctant to take risks on behalf of

00:26:41.279 --> 00:26:46.940
these negotiations, that it's not clear to
them why it's in their interest to really

00:26:46.940 --> 00:26:54.049
put their cards on the table at this stage
for the reasons that I described before in

00:26:54.049 --> 00:26:55.380
part, that their timelines are quite different.

00:26:55.380 --> 00:26:59.510
We're in a rush because we would like this
all to be wrapped up with a -- with a bow

00:26:59.510 --> 00:27:06.850
in time for 2014, but that doesn't make -- that
doesn't align with Pakistani perceptions.

00:27:06.850 --> 00:27:17.809
You know, I think there are easy metrics to
test Pakistani decision-making about these

00:27:17.809 --> 00:27:18.809
negotiations.

00:27:18.809 --> 00:27:23.970
They've -- as I pointed out in The New Yorker
piece, they've remained silent on the desirability

00:27:23.970 --> 00:27:25.919
of the negotiations.

00:27:25.919 --> 00:27:29.420
They could issue a statement saying, we think
these are a good idea; we think the Taliban

00:27:29.420 --> 00:27:31.090
ought to participate.

00:27:31.090 --> 00:27:39.740
They could release some important Taliban
figures who are in custody in Pakistan, (the

00:27:39.740 --> 00:27:45.990
"Liberator" ?) being the most notable of those,
and perhaps they will over time.

00:27:45.990 --> 00:27:53.570
I think that this is not a static stalemate
between the United States and Pakistan.

00:27:53.570 --> 00:28:01.120
I think the next -- I think we've possibly
touched bottom in the last cycle.

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:06.000
I don't want to be too bold to predict that
because every time I see my friends, who I've

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:11.400
been in touch with in -- about Pakistan since
the late '80s, they always -- this one friend

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:15.710
of mine has the repeated line, the good news
is next year it'll be worse.

00:28:15.710 --> 00:28:16.710
(Laughter.)

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:19.529
And he's been right for about 20 years.

00:28:19.529 --> 00:28:25.210
And -- but there's -- there -- there's a sense
that, on both sides, there is activity under

00:28:25.210 --> 00:28:29.090
way, one step at a time, to at least clear
the brush.

00:28:29.090 --> 00:28:34.419
Husain Haqqani is out of Pakistan; that matter
is resolved.

00:28:34.419 --> 00:28:41.040
The crisis between the army and Gilani has
been smoothed over.

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:51.059
The negotiations over transit are under sail
and will probably be resolved.

00:28:51.059 --> 00:28:52.860
And outreach is going on.

00:28:52.860 --> 00:28:58.929
So I think, over the next six or eight months,
there will be an effort on both sides to try

00:28:58.929 --> 00:29:05.019
to define a limited agenda of shared interests
in which the stability of Afghanistan would

00:29:05.019 --> 00:29:13.610
loom as an obvious subject because it is in
Pakistan's interest to preventAfghanistan

00:29:13.610 --> 00:29:14.610
from cracking up.

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:19.490
There is no doubt that if Afghanistan cracks
up, Pakistan will suffer even more than it

00:29:19.490 --> 00:29:23.620
already has from the violence and conflict
in the region and the United States would

00:29:23.620 --> 00:29:30.679
also wish to construct a transition in which
Pakistan remains stable and intact as a state

00:29:30.679 --> 00:29:35.460
and so that is a pretty powerful common agenda.

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:40.200
It would be irrational if the two sides could
not find a way back to it.

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:44.880
Whether negotiations with the Taliban are
best understood as the central element of

00:29:44.880 --> 00:29:46.460
that shared agenda or not, I'm not --

00:29:46.460 --> 00:29:47.460
MR.

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:48.460
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS (?): Yeah.

00:29:48.460 --> 00:29:49.460
&gt;&gt;COLL: -- I'm not so sure.

00:29:49.460 --> 00:29:50.460
MR.

00:29:50.460 --> 00:29:52.161
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS (?): There's one other issue I
want your thoughts on, and then we're going

00:29:52.161 --> 00:29:56.460
to turn it over to the audience so you can
ask your own questions.

00:29:56.460 --> 00:30:03.190
But, over the last 10 years, the one enduring
principle guiding U.S. policy with respect

00:30:03.190 --> 00:30:10.440
to Afghanistan has been the determination
to deny, dismantle and defeat al-Qaida.

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:16.409
Of course in the eve of -- and you were there,
Bob -- in the eve of the -- going to war in

00:30:16.409 --> 00:30:22.650
Afghanistan, the Taliban leadership in power
at that time did have the opportunity to avoid

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:28.399
war by simply getting the al-Qaida out of
Afghanistan.

00:30:28.399 --> 00:30:32.660
And now when we talk about these negotiations,
there's a lot of confusion about what the

00:30:32.660 --> 00:30:34.550
red lines of the United States are.

00:30:34.550 --> 00:30:40.940
But one pretty clear red line is that the
Taliban would have to sever their relations

00:30:40.940 --> 00:30:42.200
with al-Qaida.

00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:47.889
I'm curious, among all three of you, where
do you see al-Qaida right now in this mix?

00:30:47.889 --> 00:30:50.049
Bob, do you want to start?

00:30:50.049 --> 00:30:55.640
&gt;&gt;GRENIER: Well, I think, in a way, al-Qaida
is a little bit peripheral in the context

00:30:55.640 --> 00:31:02.059
of the dynamics on the ground and among ourselves,
the Pakistanis and the Afghans.

00:31:02.059 --> 00:31:07.669
And yes, absolutely, it would be a red line
for us that the Taliban would have to, in

00:31:07.669 --> 00:31:11.889
some way, you know, formally, you know, cut
ties with al-Qaida.

00:31:11.889 --> 00:31:14.789
The problem is that I'm just not sure what
that would mean.

00:31:14.789 --> 00:31:22.700
I mean, at the end of the day, from the Taliban's
perspective, these are good Muslims who are

00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:31.929
behaving in the right way, who are coming
to us and asking for our aid and succor.

00:31:31.929 --> 00:31:36.049
And they tend to see things -- I mean, we
hear that this is not your father's Taliban

00:31:36.049 --> 00:31:40.050
and that they've evolved in terms of their
thinking, but it's hard for me to imagine

00:31:40.050 --> 00:31:44.380
them being put in that situation where they
wouldn't see the question in a very binary

00:31:44.380 --> 00:31:46.049
way.

00:31:46.049 --> 00:31:50.480
And the correct answer, by their likes, is,
well, of course; you are welcome.

00:31:50.480 --> 00:31:52.450
Now, they might try to put limits on them.

00:31:52.450 --> 00:31:53.870
We've seen what that looks like before.

00:31:53.870 --> 00:31:58.970
Given the weakness of al-Qaida, what I've
just said may not matter all that much, particularly

00:31:58.970 --> 00:32:05.850
if there is a continuing unilateral U.S. role
in Afghanistan so we can control that situation

00:32:05.850 --> 00:32:10.440
with al-Qaida should it get out of hand in
certain localities.

00:32:10.440 --> 00:32:14.809
But at the end of the day, I just don't see
what those sorts of promises from the Taliban

00:32:14.809 --> 00:32:16.539
would really mean in a practical way.

00:32:16.539 --> 00:32:21.240
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Well, Dan, if in fact al-Qaida
is peripheral to this -- to this situation,

00:32:21.240 --> 00:32:23.250
I mean, what does that imply for the United
States?

00:32:23.250 --> 00:32:27.320
Because every time you ask a U.S. official,
what's the justification for our involvement

00:32:27.320 --> 00:32:28.980
there, al-Qaida comes up.

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:36.830
&gt;&gt;MARKEY: Well, I think how a U.S. official
justifies our involvement in the region on

00:32:36.830 --> 00:32:40.409
the basis of al-Qaida has something to do
with American politics and so on.

00:32:40.409 --> 00:32:45.450
And -- but the -- let me -- let me take a
slightly different stab at the al-Qaida.

00:32:45.450 --> 00:32:50.040
I wouldn't say that al-Qaida is peripheral,
although it is significantly diminished.

00:32:50.040 --> 00:32:56.149
What I would also observe is something that's
-- getting back to the report from a year

00:32:56.149 --> 00:33:00.000
ago, pieces of it are, I think, somewhat dated.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:01.870
So much has happened over the year.

00:33:01.870 --> 00:33:06.940
But one of the pieces that, looking back,
I thought was useful and important --

00:33:06.940 --> 00:33:08.430
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: You're talking about the task
force report?

00:33:08.430 --> 00:33:16.179
&gt;&gt;MARKEY: -- the task force report, yeah -- is
the -- its focus on LeT, Lashkar-e-Taiba.

00:33:16.179 --> 00:33:22.070
Not all assessments of the regional threat
from Pakistan and Afghanistan, certainly focusing

00:33:22.070 --> 00:33:27.090
more on Pakistan, really emphasize this as
an issue.

00:33:27.090 --> 00:33:33.529
And the U.S. government has certainly ramped
up its concern and some of its rhetoric about

00:33:33.529 --> 00:33:37.929
LeT, particularly after the 2008 Mumbai attack.

00:33:37.929 --> 00:33:42.800
And yet if you look at the region and you
look at al-Qaida as increasingly a diminished

00:33:42.800 --> 00:33:48.250
or spent force, the question is whether you're
likely to see follow-on groups, organizations

00:33:48.250 --> 00:33:53.769
that are still motivated to undertake similar
types of attacks in the region and globally.

00:33:53.769 --> 00:33:59.990
If you had to put your finger on any of the
groups that might be the one, today it's LeT.

00:33:59.990 --> 00:34:07.970
And its continuing ties or apparent ties with
the Pakistani state make it exceptionally

00:34:07.970 --> 00:34:12.180
dangerous and in some ways unique.

00:34:12.180 --> 00:34:19.140
And even over the past few weeks you've seen
stories about its sort of -- he would say

00:34:19.140 --> 00:34:24.210
he's no longer the leader of LeT, but Hafiz
Saeed -- you've seen some stories about increasing

00:34:24.210 --> 00:34:29.320
public appearances, demonstrations.

00:34:29.320 --> 00:34:33.360
This is an organization that is still strong,
still active.

00:34:33.360 --> 00:34:40.190
And if we keep -- if we take our eye off that
ball, even if we have seriously decimated

00:34:40.190 --> 00:34:45.260
al-Qaida, I think we will be underestimating
the nature of the threat -- the terrorist

00:34:45.260 --> 00:34:49.610
threat from the region that will persist for
a significant period of time.

00:34:49.610 --> 00:34:53.690
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Steve, do you agree with that,
that al-Qaida is really no longer the story

00:34:53.690 --> 00:34:56.400
that it was and that other actors are more
important?

00:34:56.400 --> 00:34:59.920
&gt;&gt;COLL: Well, I mean, let's remind ourselves
of the -- of the background.

00:34:59.920 --> 00:35:07.670
By 2005-2006 al-Qaida in the tribal areas
had become a revived international menace

00:35:07.670 --> 00:35:16.332
-- the planes-bombing conspiracy of 2006,
multiple attacks in Britain, the Zazi case,

00:35:16.332 --> 00:35:21.630
up -- moving forward through 2007-2008 after
the Red Mosque, when even more radicals flushed

00:35:21.630 --> 00:35:28.500
out and went up to the tribal regions, there
was a gathering there of international capacity

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:32.250
that al-Qaida had put together circa that
period.

00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:37.260
Then the United States started pounding them
and has pounded them pretty relentlessly for

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:43.481
a period of two or three years, starting toward
the tail end of the Bush administration, but

00:35:43.481 --> 00:35:45.660
picking up intensively in the Obama administration.

00:35:45.660 --> 00:35:48.900
And that has had a big impact on them.

00:35:48.900 --> 00:35:52.940
They've obviously -- culminating in bin Laden's
killing last May.

00:35:52.940 --> 00:35:58.520
Now, there is a -- there is an interesting
question, which is if al-Qaida is diminished

00:35:58.520 --> 00:36:03.360
to the point that it is no longer a global
strategic threat, how would we ever recognize

00:36:03.360 --> 00:36:05.640
that; what would the implications be?

00:36:05.640 --> 00:36:12.290
And I think the critical question is are there
individuals, groups, networks who still intend

00:36:12.290 --> 00:36:18.450
to carry out attacks outside the region, and
that there will probably always be some who

00:36:18.450 --> 00:36:21.020
-- about whom that can be said.

00:36:21.020 --> 00:36:25.100
They may be splinters from groups like Lashkar
and Jaish-e-Muhammad and some of the sectarian

00:36:25.100 --> 00:36:26.100
groups.

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:28.290
That's the form of al-Qaida that probably
has the most durability.

00:36:28.290 --> 00:36:32.470
You know, in Afghanistan, al-Qaida really
is peripheral.

00:36:32.470 --> 00:36:37.340
I mean, it maybe a hundred, 200 people, and
that's being generous to the Uzbeks and others

00:36:37.340 --> 00:36:39.900
who fly under the al-Qaida flag.

00:36:39.900 --> 00:36:44.330
And the interrogations of the -- of the Taliban,
though you know, obviously, it's in the interest

00:36:44.330 --> 00:36:48.290
of detained Taliban to -- they know what the
Americans want to hear about al-Qaida, so

00:36:48.290 --> 00:36:49.870
they distance themselves.

00:36:49.870 --> 00:36:54.740
But I had this interesting experience in December
where I traveled independently in Kandahar,

00:36:54.740 --> 00:36:56.100
so I had to kind of dress local.

00:36:56.100 --> 00:37:01.540
And all my Kandahari friends were very anxious
that I look authentic so that I didn't attract

00:37:01.540 --> 00:37:03.571
attention to the cars we were riding around
in.

00:37:03.571 --> 00:37:08.190
And I remember I went shopping in Kabul to
get all of my Kandahari fashions right.

00:37:08.190 --> 00:37:09.190
And --

00:37:09.190 --> 00:37:10.190
MR.

00:37:10.190 --> 00:37:11.190
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS (?): Where'd you find a beard?

00:37:11.190 --> 00:37:12.250
&gt;&gt;COLL: Well, so I -- well, I did try.

00:37:12.250 --> 00:37:15.930
And -- (laughter) -- but I remember -- I remember
-- I remember the night before I flew down,

00:37:15.930 --> 00:37:20.080
I looked in the mirror and I said, OK, I do
not look like a Kandahari at all, but I do

00:37:20.080 --> 00:37:22.750
look like a Syrian al-Qaida facilitator.

00:37:22.750 --> 00:37:23.880
(Laughter.)

00:37:23.880 --> 00:37:31.530
And in fact, on the ground, riding around,
I presented as basically al-Qaida to -- because

00:37:31.530 --> 00:37:37.330
I was -- looked -- blended dresswise, but
fair -- and all of my Kandahari friends and

00:37:37.330 --> 00:37:41.430
contacts and everyone I dealt with said, you
watch; as soon as anyone picks you up, they're

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:43.080
going to turn and run in the other direction.

00:37:43.080 --> 00:37:47.980
Nobody wants anything to do with al-Qaida
because it is nothing but trouble.

00:37:47.980 --> 00:37:51.810
And it was actually a very useful position
for me to be in.

00:37:51.810 --> 00:37:52.810
(Laughter.)

00:37:52.810 --> 00:37:53.810
(Chuckles.)

00:37:53.810 --> 00:37:54.810
Just turn and look the other way.

00:37:54.810 --> 00:38:01.560
But yeah, so I -- and I did not -- I have
not heard in years, outside of a few little

00:38:01.560 --> 00:38:06.470
places where -- certainly not in the south
-- of any significant al-Qaida presence in

00:38:06.470 --> 00:38:07.470
Afghanistan.

00:38:07.470 --> 00:38:08.470
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Yeah.

00:38:08.470 --> 00:38:13.390
Well, I'm curious what some of you might think
about the implications of al-Qaida being such

00:38:13.390 --> 00:38:17.750
a diminished sort of part of the narrative
in South Asia right now.

00:38:17.750 --> 00:38:22.370
I'm been dragging these guys hither and yon
all over the strategic landscape in South

00:38:22.370 --> 00:38:23.370
Asia.

00:38:23.370 --> 00:38:26.940
Maybe you folks can focus this discussion
a little bit more.

00:38:26.940 --> 00:38:29.940
So this is your chance.

00:38:29.940 --> 00:38:31.060
Raise your hands.

00:38:31.060 --> 00:38:32.180
Identify yourself.

00:38:32.180 --> 00:38:33.650
Wait for the microphone.

00:38:33.650 --> 00:38:36.820
You know the drill.

00:38:36.820 --> 00:38:38.410
Ambassador Schaffer.

00:38:38.410 --> 00:38:39.520
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Thank you.

00:38:39.520 --> 00:38:41.430
Tezi Schaffer, Brookings.

00:38:41.430 --> 00:38:49.630
I'm troubled by all three presentations because
I think all three of them dramatically underplay

00:38:49.630 --> 00:38:55.400
the conflict in strategic objectives between
the United States and Pakistan.

00:38:55.400 --> 00:39:00.531
I don't think the Pakistanisare ambivalent
about U.S.-Taliban negotiations; I think they

00:39:00.531 --> 00:39:02.440
want to be in charge.

00:39:02.440 --> 00:39:08.250
I don't think they're unwilling to take risks
for an outcome in Afghanistan that the U.S.

00:39:08.250 --> 00:39:09.650
would find acceptable.

00:39:09.650 --> 00:39:11.520
I think that's not what's important to them.

00:39:11.520 --> 00:39:15.990
What's important to them is being the dominant
power and freezing out India.

00:39:15.990 --> 00:39:23.040
The key question facing the U.S., it seems
to me, is, is Pakistan willing and able to

00:39:23.040 --> 00:39:27.940
reach some kind of strategic agreement with
the United States?

00:39:27.940 --> 00:39:33.510
I am deeply skeptical that this is the case
now, not just because of all the emotion churned

00:39:33.510 --> 00:39:41.110
up in the past year, but also because as time
has gone on, these strategic conflicts have

00:39:41.110 --> 00:39:48.030
become deeper and edgier, so that a year or
two ago, there would've been a strong case

00:39:48.030 --> 00:39:55.190
to make for the U.S. humoring Pakistan's sensitivities
in Afghanistan in the hopes of getting agreement

00:39:55.190 --> 00:40:00.660
on some outcome we could all live with; I
think that case is much weaker now.

00:40:00.660 --> 00:40:06.760
And I think is the big problem the U.S. faces,
plus the fact that Pakistan is a bigger prize

00:40:06.760 --> 00:40:07.760
than Afghanistan.

00:40:07.760 --> 00:40:11.350
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Who wants to take a crack at that?

00:40:11.350 --> 00:40:12.350
&gt;&gt;MR.

00:40:12.350 --> 00:40:14.010
GRENIER (?): Well, I agree with Tezi.

00:40:14.010 --> 00:40:15.010
(Chuckles.)

00:40:15.010 --> 00:40:17.840
In fact, I think that that's absolutely right.

00:40:17.840 --> 00:40:23.660
I think that the Pakistanis are focused like
a laser beam on their own interests.

00:40:23.660 --> 00:40:29.930
And under these ambiguous circumstances, they
have no assurances that their interests will

00:40:29.930 --> 00:40:35.920
be taken into account by any process which
is led by the United States.

00:40:35.920 --> 00:40:42.281
And I think that you're right, that the influence
of India in Afghanistan figures very, very

00:40:42.281 --> 00:40:46.150
heavily within the Pakistani calculation.

00:40:46.150 --> 00:40:51.180
And so I think that if there is any hope for
us to reach some sort of a strategic accommodation

00:40:51.180 --> 00:40:52.690
there, I think we have to realize a couple
of things.

00:40:52.690 --> 00:40:59.061
One is that, as much as we would like to sort
of have the equal involvement of all of the

00:40:59.061 --> 00:41:03.980
-- of the neighbors of Afghanistan, in fact,
there is one neighbor that counts much more

00:41:03.980 --> 00:41:05.820
than all the others, and that's Pakistan.

00:41:05.820 --> 00:41:12.050
It's just a reality and something that we
have to accommodate, at least to some degree.

00:41:12.050 --> 00:41:21.730
I think that it's also true, therefore, that
any achievable accommodation in Afghanistan

00:41:21.730 --> 00:41:27.770
that could have some support from the Pakistani
side would require a great deal of difficult

00:41:27.770 --> 00:41:34.220
negotiation between ourselves -- well, I guess,
amongst ourselves, the Afghans -- if you will,

00:41:34.220 --> 00:41:37.040
the Northern Alliance-dominated Afghans -- and
India.

00:41:37.040 --> 00:41:42.700
And it's easy for me to sit here and say that
it would be a grand exercise in enlightened

00:41:42.700 --> 00:41:47.290
self-interest if the Indians were to pull
back to some degree in Afghanistan.

00:41:47.290 --> 00:41:51.600
It's going to be very, very difficult to convince
them of that, however.

00:41:51.600 --> 00:41:57.820
But the other real trump in all of this is
that, you know, it would be nice if, at the

00:41:57.820 --> 00:42:03.210
end of the day, we could reach some sort of
an -- of an accommodation with the Afghan

00:42:03.210 --> 00:42:08.100
parties and with the Pakistanis, which we
might not particularly like, but we'd swallow

00:42:08.100 --> 00:42:13.890
hard and agree to if the Pakistanis could
deliver.

00:42:13.890 --> 00:42:16.980
And I just don't think that they can deliver.

00:42:16.980 --> 00:42:22.710
I mean, I sort of fantasize about havingsort
of a Bashar al-Assad-type conversation with

00:42:22.710 --> 00:42:28.950
the Afghan Taliban where he sits down and
says, look, you know, you're my brothers.

00:42:28.950 --> 00:42:33.310
And we are with you, and we want to do everything
we possibly can for you, but this is the best

00:42:33.310 --> 00:42:34.310
we can do.

00:42:34.310 --> 00:42:37.870
And it's up to you whether you accept it,
but you should accept it.

00:42:37.870 --> 00:42:42.110
There's no one in Pakistan who can have that
kind of conversation with them, and that,

00:42:42.110 --> 00:42:44.170
to me, is what scotches the whole thing.

00:42:44.170 --> 00:42:47.410
If the Pakistanis could actually deliver at
the end of the day, we could use some kind

00:42:47.410 --> 00:42:48.410
of a deal.

00:42:48.410 --> 00:42:49.410
I just don't see how they can deliver.

00:42:49.410 --> 00:42:53.060
&gt;&gt;COLL: But you know, I would just add -- I
mean, Pakistan has a record of miscalculation

00:42:53.060 --> 00:42:57.520
in Afghanistan that is just appalling and
persistent.

00:42:57.520 --> 00:43:03.780
It must reflect in a failure to understand
Afghanistan adequately to manage Pakistan's

00:43:03.780 --> 00:43:05.250
own interests there.

00:43:05.250 --> 00:43:07.750
They've picked the wrong clients again and
again.

00:43:07.750 --> 00:43:13.260
They -- they'll lecture Americans about how
Afghanistan is, but in fact their understanding

00:43:13.260 --> 00:43:18.460
of Afghanistan's internal dynamics, factions
and decision-making is misguided.

00:43:18.460 --> 00:43:23.780
They misapprehend India's intention and presence
in the country to a great degree.

00:43:23.780 --> 00:43:29.050
And they make -- they've made strategic judgments
on the basis of bad analysis.

00:43:29.050 --> 00:43:34.150
Now, I think there is some recognition in
Pakistan -- quiet, unadmitted -- I don't -- I

00:43:34.150 --> 00:43:39.690
-- which shouldn't be a requirement that they
come forward and confess these errors publicly;

00:43:39.690 --> 00:43:41.770
they're written into history.

00:43:41.770 --> 00:43:48.060
I think there's a recognition, at least in
some quarters, that these mistakes are grave

00:43:48.060 --> 00:43:51.620
and part of a pattern of overreaching.

00:43:51.620 --> 00:43:56.070
And there's -- you know, there's a sort of
tentative search for, well, what would a Pakistani

00:43:56.070 --> 00:44:00.120
strategy in Afghanistan that wasn't overreaching
look like?

00:44:00.120 --> 00:44:05.900
And I haven't heard an articulation of that
from the Pakistani side that any Afghan I

00:44:05.900 --> 00:44:10.970
know in a position of authority would recognize
as a plausible basis for a settlement.

00:44:10.970 --> 00:44:16.970
So, at best, you could say we're at the very
beginning of a new imagination.

00:44:16.970 --> 00:44:21.430
But then you look at Pakistani conduct, and
Pakistani conduct is the "same old, same old"

00:44:21.430 --> 00:44:22.430
--

00:44:22.430 --> 00:44:23.430
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Now you --

00:44:23.430 --> 00:44:24.430
&gt;&gt;COLL: -- whether it's out of weakness or
aspiration.

00:44:24.430 --> 00:44:27.690
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Well, you said before that they
speak -- that they think in the long term;

00:44:27.690 --> 00:44:32.890
yet in some ways it seems like they have a
very short-term perspective and kind of, as

00:44:32.890 --> 00:44:38.170
you've all agreed, kind of lack strategic
sort of depth in their thinking sometimes.

00:44:38.170 --> 00:44:45.790
&gt;&gt;COLL: Yeah, they have a language of strategy
and certainly enduring interests and an acute

00:44:45.790 --> 00:44:52.740
sense of prerogative about Pakistan's national
security, you know, which is -- which is the

00:44:52.740 --> 00:44:56.760
-- justified in any sovereign country in principle.

00:44:56.760 --> 00:45:07.090
But as to Afghanistan, they have proceeded
from flawed assumptions about the pliability

00:45:07.090 --> 00:45:12.940
of Afghanistan to their strategy, about who
-- what clients are durable, about the nature

00:45:12.940 --> 00:45:17.100
of power- sharing in Kabul, about the enmity
of the Northern Alliance.

00:45:17.100 --> 00:45:21.510
I mean, they've miscalculated on big questions
again and again.

00:45:21.510 --> 00:45:25.430
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Chris.

00:45:25.430 --> 00:45:29.350
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Thanks.

00:45:29.350 --> 00:45:30.400
Chris Isham from CBS.

00:45:30.400 --> 00:45:34.339
I think we're kind of dancing around something
here, and I wonder -- maybe the land of fantasy

00:45:34.339 --> 00:45:40.450
-- it's unclear to me what kind of a deal
might possibly exist.

00:45:40.450 --> 00:45:45.480
What kind of a deal could we negotiate with
the Taliban, with Pakistan, as their supporters,

00:45:45.480 --> 00:45:48.650
that could even be conceivably realistic?

00:45:48.650 --> 00:45:56.770
I mean, India out, girls' education, participation
in the democratic process -- I mean, you know,

00:45:56.770 --> 00:46:00.620
what kind of a deal, even in -- even in the
fantasy world do you -- do the three of you

00:46:00.620 --> 00:46:05.601
think could you possibly put on the table
that might have a chance of getting off the

00:46:05.601 --> 00:46:06.601
ground?

00:46:06.601 --> 00:46:09.760
And also considering that the unpopularity
of the Taliban, which continues -- we continue

00:46:09.760 --> 00:46:11.060
to see in the polling.

00:46:11.060 --> 00:46:12.420
&gt;&gt;COLL: Well, I mean --

00:46:12.420 --> 00:46:13.450
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Go ahead.

00:46:13.450 --> 00:46:14.450
MR.

00:46:14.450 --> 00:46:15.450
: Sorry.

00:46:15.450 --> 00:46:18.660
&gt;&gt;COLL: Well, I mean, it's just worth pointing
out there are former Taliban sitting in Parliament

00:46:18.660 --> 00:46:19.660
now.

00:46:19.660 --> 00:46:21.670
There are former HezB (ph) sitting in Parliament
now.

00:46:21.670 --> 00:46:25.520
History is full of insurgent movements that
converted to politics.

00:46:25.520 --> 00:46:30.170
So the idea generally would be to convert
the Taliban into a political party or some

00:46:30.170 --> 00:46:33.000
section of the Taliban into a political party.

00:46:33.000 --> 00:46:37.450
Obviously not every Taliban under arms -- and
perhaps not even half of Taliban under arms

00:46:37.450 --> 00:46:45.370
-- would follow such an agreement, but to
reduce violence, to sustain the state in Afghanistan,

00:46:45.370 --> 00:46:51.210
it's certainly conceivable that a section
of Taliban could be persuaded to enter politics.

00:46:51.210 --> 00:46:58.310
That's the -- I'm not offering odds; I'm just
saying that's the conceivable framework.

00:46:58.310 --> 00:47:04.320
And then power-sharing in the center, but
not control; it -- devolved administration

00:47:04.320 --> 00:47:10.520
in which former Taliban are governors of provinces
where they've historically been influential.

00:47:10.520 --> 00:47:12.330
Those are the sorts of things that people
talk about.

00:47:12.330 --> 00:47:13.640
Even Afghans talk about that.

00:47:13.640 --> 00:47:18.920
Afghans can imagine a stable -- a semistable
power-sharing arrangement.

00:47:18.920 --> 00:47:24.160
They've already negotiated such an arrangement
with weakperipheral members of the Taliban.

00:47:24.160 --> 00:47:27.420
They know how to make those deals sustainable.

00:47:27.420 --> 00:47:31.930
But whether a strategic partnership of that
type is conceivable, that's another matter.

00:47:31.930 --> 00:47:33.330
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Go ahead -- go.

00:47:33.330 --> 00:47:34.330
MR.

00:47:34.330 --> 00:47:35.330
: All right.

00:47:35.330 --> 00:47:38.880
&gt;&gt;MARKEY: I would only make the observation
that it doesn't have to be a grand bargain

00:47:38.880 --> 00:47:42.030
that everybody's included, and I think you're
alluding to this.

00:47:42.030 --> 00:47:48.160
The process of a reconciliation dialogue can
be primarily geared towards a narrower goal

00:47:48.160 --> 00:47:54.850
of peeling elements of the Taliban away, those
who are willing in some way to be co-opted

00:47:54.850 --> 00:47:58.700
or encouraged to be a part of some sort of
future Afghan politics.

00:47:58.700 --> 00:48:05.280
And the rest can be further driven to sort
of the irreconcilable category, and then dealt

00:48:05.280 --> 00:48:07.360
with in other ways.

00:48:07.360 --> 00:48:13.250
I think a broader observation, though: I'm
also quite skeptical about the direction that

00:48:13.250 --> 00:48:18.960
our reconciliation agenda is taking, but for
a different reason and a -- and a reason that

00:48:18.960 --> 00:48:23.900
has, I guess, more to do with Pakistan than
with Afghanistan -- although I'm -- I don't

00:48:23.900 --> 00:48:26.880
know how a lot of Afghans would accept any
kind of reconciliation.

00:48:26.880 --> 00:48:31.850
But just on the Pakistan side, the message
that we send to Pakistan, particularly when

00:48:31.850 --> 00:48:38.710
our reconciliation dialogue is increasingly
open to all members of the Taliban, including

00:48:38.710 --> 00:48:44.340
the Haqqani network -- or at least apparently
that way -- is that their strategy, their

00:48:44.340 --> 00:48:49.010
connections with these groups over the past
decade that we have harped on again and again

00:48:49.010 --> 00:48:54.160
and again as being dangerous both to them
and to us and to the region -- their strategy,

00:48:54.160 --> 00:48:58.530
in their mind, looks like it's working; that
we're coming around -- and I've heard exactly

00:48:58.530 --> 00:49:01.690
this -- that we're coming around to their
vision of the problem, which is that all of

00:49:01.690 --> 00:49:05.910
these groups can be incorporated and all of
them should have a role to play in Afghanistan,

00:49:05.910 --> 00:49:10.460
no matter what blood is on their hands or
what their -- the nature of their previous

00:49:10.460 --> 00:49:13.760
relationship with al-Qaida may or may not
have been.

00:49:13.760 --> 00:49:19.170
As we come closer to that, I think we send
-- it's a missed opportunity, in a sense,

00:49:19.170 --> 00:49:24.350
for what you might call a learning moment
for Pakistan to see the danger of its connections

00:49:24.350 --> 00:49:26.210
with these types of organizations.

00:49:26.210 --> 00:49:32.190
And as I mentioned Lashkar-e-Taiba before,
the only sort of logical conclusion that you

00:49:32.190 --> 00:49:37.710
might reach out of all of this is that connections
with militants work; it is a valuable and

00:49:37.710 --> 00:49:39.860
effective tool for regional diplomacy.

00:49:39.860 --> 00:49:44.620
And if you're a Pakistani, you might have
good reasons to hold onto those tools into

00:49:44.620 --> 00:49:49.590
the future, even when they are exceedingly
difficult to manage and exceedingly dangerous

00:49:49.590 --> 00:49:55.000
to Pakistan itself and certainly court the
possibility of a future conflict between Pakistan

00:49:55.000 --> 00:49:57.290
and us, between Pakistan and India, and so
on.

00:49:57.290 --> 00:50:01.060
So I'm worried about reconciliation more for
that reason.

00:50:01.060 --> 00:50:07.860
And I believe that our management of the process
and who's in and who's out is meaningful and

00:50:07.860 --> 00:50:13.200
interpreted in important ways by Pakistanis
for that reason.

00:50:13.200 --> 00:50:20.380
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Yes, to you, ma'am -- you, yeah.

00:50:20.380 --> 00:50:25.510
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Avis Bohlen, retired diplomat.

00:50:25.510 --> 00:50:33.800
I -- I'm sort of puzzled by something I'm
hearing several of you saying, which is that

00:50:33.800 --> 00:50:40.090
Pakistan doesn't understand its own interests
in this -- in this situation; which implies

00:50:40.090 --> 00:50:47.410
that somehow we have understood what their
interests are and are -- you know, are sort

00:50:47.410 --> 00:50:53.100
of looking at it from up above and can understand
what their greater -- what their greater interest

00:50:53.100 --> 00:50:54.510
is.

00:50:54.510 --> 00:50:57.910
That doesn't really make a lot of sense to
me.

00:50:57.910 --> 00:51:03.090
I mean, I think we've -- for every miscalculation
that Pakistan may have made in Afghanistan,

00:51:03.090 --> 00:51:07.060
we've made about 10.

00:51:07.060 --> 00:51:14.140
Why is it impossible that they should -- as
you've said, there's a longer timeline, and

00:51:14.140 --> 00:51:19.950
they're looking at a longer situation and
how it will evolve over time.

00:51:19.950 --> 00:51:25.960
Why is that not a perfectly valid definition
of their own interest, which may not be very

00:51:25.960 --> 00:51:28.920
much in line with our own?

00:51:28.920 --> 00:51:31.530
And just a second short question.

00:51:31.530 --> 00:51:37.230
Is India's interest in Afghanistan strategic
or opportunistic, economic?

00:51:37.230 --> 00:51:39.570
What's its game?

00:51:39.570 --> 00:51:46.210
&gt;&gt;GRENIER: Well, maybe I could just address
the first part of that.

00:51:46.210 --> 00:51:47.410
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Yeah.

00:51:47.410 --> 00:51:51.400
&gt;&gt;GRENIER: And I guess -- I guess what I would
say is that even if the combined genius of

00:51:51.400 --> 00:51:57.080
the people on this stage is able to perceive
Pakistani interests in Afghanistan better

00:51:57.080 --> 00:52:01.810
than they can -- even if we accept that -- it
doesn't matter, because at the end of the

00:52:01.810 --> 00:52:05.310
day, Pakistanis are going to follow their
interests as they perceive them.

00:52:05.310 --> 00:52:07.550
We're going to have to play that ball where
it lies.

00:52:07.550 --> 00:52:09.870
And, yes, it can be moved, perhaps.

00:52:09.870 --> 00:52:17.610
They are not -- they are not completely immune
to some types of suasion -- not pressure,

00:52:17.610 --> 00:52:18.610
but suasion.

00:52:18.610 --> 00:52:22.350
But at the end of the day, Pakistani views
of their interests in Afghanistan are what

00:52:22.350 --> 00:52:23.990
they are, and we're going to have to deal
with them as they are.

00:52:23.990 --> 00:52:27.650
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: One of the points that you've
made, Bob, is that this whole idea of carrots-and-stick

00:52:27.650 --> 00:52:32.130
and the transactional sort of approach just
doesn't work with Pakistan.

00:52:32.130 --> 00:52:35.240
&gt;&gt;GRENIER: Well, I think that that raises
another question.

00:52:35.240 --> 00:52:40.250
And that is that at the start of the -- of
this administration, I was one of the ones

00:52:40.250 --> 00:52:45.640
in one of the many studies who said, look,
we have to get away from this transactional

00:52:45.640 --> 00:52:52.650
dynamic here; we really need to develop some
more of a strategic approach to our relations

00:52:52.650 --> 00:52:54.590
with Pakistan.

00:52:54.590 --> 00:52:58.810
You know, we'll -- for the long term, we'll
-- I think we've reached a point now where

00:52:58.810 --> 00:53:01.940
any kind of a transactional relationship would
actually look pretty good.

00:53:01.940 --> 00:53:02.940
(Laughter.)

00:53:02.940 --> 00:53:08.210
So I think that the best we can hope for,
at least in the short term, is, yes, a transactional

00:53:08.210 --> 00:53:12.720
relationship, but a transactional relationship
that's focused on deals that are actually

00:53:12.720 --> 00:53:13.720
makeable.

00:53:13.720 --> 00:53:14.720
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Steve?

00:53:14.720 --> 00:53:17.150
&gt;&gt;COLL: Well, let me just clarify the point
that I was making earlier.

00:53:17.150 --> 00:53:22.970
I think Pakistan has a record of failing to
achieve its own objectives in Afghanistan.

00:53:22.970 --> 00:53:27.100
After the withdrawal of the Soviet Union,
it pursued a (coup-making ?) strategy to seize

00:53:27.100 --> 00:53:32.440
control of Kabul through Gulbuddin Hekmatyar's
forces and failed to do that.

00:53:32.440 --> 00:53:38.300
Afterwards it sought to control Afghanistan
as a -- as a source of strategic depth, as

00:53:38.300 --> 00:53:40.210
Pakistani generals put it, through the Taliban.

00:53:40.210 --> 00:53:44.450
The -- even after they installed the Taliban
in power in Afghanistan, that proved not to

00:53:44.450 --> 00:53:49.580
be a durable strategy because the Taliban
were not in fact able to carry Pakistan's

00:53:49.580 --> 00:53:51.800
interests in the way that the army had conceived.

00:53:51.800 --> 00:53:54.990
That's the reason that I think they have miscalculated
again and again.

00:53:54.990 --> 00:54:01.320
I certainly agree that whatever they -- you
know, that the sovereign state of Pakistan

00:54:01.320 --> 00:54:03.340
will decide its own interests for itself.

00:54:03.340 --> 00:54:07.450
And countries, like people, have a funny way
of not bending to other people's perceptions

00:54:07.450 --> 00:54:09.220
of what their interests are.

00:54:09.220 --> 00:54:18.020
The -- I think, however, that history of failure
is important because it was created by a narrow

00:54:18.020 --> 00:54:23.030
set of institutions in Pakistani national
life, the army and the intelligence services,

00:54:23.030 --> 00:54:27.850
and that the policies that would be constructed
by a Pakistan that enjoyed more normal civil-military

00:54:27.850 --> 00:54:34.150
relations and, indeed, more normal civilian
control over foreign policy, would be far

00:54:34.150 --> 00:54:35.990
more likely to succeed.

00:54:35.990 --> 00:54:40.600
And coincidentally, they would also align
better with the interests of the United States

00:54:40.600 --> 00:54:42.900
over the long run.

00:54:42.900 --> 00:54:49.340
But the record of failure speaks for itself,
and it is not a record that was created by

00:54:49.340 --> 00:54:54.230
a unified or civilian-led Pakistani government.

00:54:54.230 --> 00:54:55.410
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Dan, you had a quick point.

00:54:55.410 --> 00:54:57.400
&gt;&gt;MARKEY: Yeah, just a quick observation on
India.

00:54:57.400 --> 00:55:01.560
Yes, it does, I think, have a strategic interest
in Afghanistan.

00:55:01.560 --> 00:55:06.530
That relates to an interest that's similar
to ours, which is that they've suffered from

00:55:06.530 --> 00:55:11.060
terrorist acts that have originated in Afghanistan.

00:55:11.060 --> 00:55:16.510
They have an interest in making sure that
they don't see a return to a Taliban or Taliban-like

00:55:16.510 --> 00:55:19.330
regime that would play host to those kinds
of groups.

00:55:19.330 --> 00:55:21.310
So that's their first interest.

00:55:21.310 --> 00:55:27.990
Beyond that, some Indians have greater ambition
for more access to Central Asian -- Asia,

00:55:27.990 --> 00:55:33.460
to energy markets and so on that would only
really be possible if there was a relatively

00:55:33.460 --> 00:55:36.480
stable Afghanistan through which they could
traverse.

00:55:36.480 --> 00:55:40.680
But it would also require them tobe able to
get through Pakistan for the most part, unless

00:55:40.680 --> 00:55:42.090
they went through Iran.

00:55:42.090 --> 00:55:47.280
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: You had a question?

00:55:47.280 --> 00:55:51.730
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Hi, Pamela Constable from the
Washington Post.

00:55:51.730 --> 00:55:56.410
Just a quick point of order: Did I understand
or mishear -- is this in fact on the record?

00:55:56.410 --> 00:55:58.080
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Yes, it is on the record.

00:55:58.080 --> 00:55:59.210
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: OK, great.

00:55:59.210 --> 00:56:00.210
Thanks.

00:56:00.210 --> 00:56:02.760
I sort of wanted to change the conversation
a little bit.

00:56:02.760 --> 00:56:07.570
We've been talking a lot sort of state to
state and organization to organization and

00:56:07.570 --> 00:56:14.200
talking about al-Qaida and other groups as
sort of almost formal entities.

00:56:14.200 --> 00:56:19.960
I've tried to explore both countries sort
of more from the inside, sort of more in terms

00:56:19.960 --> 00:56:23.220
of public opinion and public sentiment.

00:56:23.220 --> 00:56:28.790
Very glad Dan talked about LeT, because I
think -- and in fact, I'd like to ask Steve

00:56:28.790 --> 00:56:33.360
about this as well, because the New America
has done some very, very good polling.

00:56:33.360 --> 00:56:37.130
LeT is a popular organization.

00:56:37.130 --> 00:56:41.450
The Taliban are a popular organization.

00:56:41.450 --> 00:56:48.980
In both of these societies, we are looking
at populations -- Muslim populations who are

00:56:48.980 --> 00:56:57.260
both more conservative, more emotionally defensive
and supportive about sort of a muscular version,

00:56:57.260 --> 00:57:03.840
if not necessarily a violent version, of Islam
than we, I think, were expecting or necessarily

00:57:03.840 --> 00:57:05.520
prepared to deal with.

00:57:05.520 --> 00:57:11.240
I think we've been focusing a lot militarily
and a lot, again, state to state, making assumptions

00:57:11.240 --> 00:57:13.730
about who's in power.

00:57:13.730 --> 00:57:23.240
These are both very weak states, including
the Pakistan army, facing increasingly opinionated,

00:57:23.240 --> 00:57:32.900
vocal and violent populations who are -- have
very, very strong feelings about Islam and

00:57:32.900 --> 00:57:34.720
are increasingly anti-Western.

00:57:34.720 --> 00:57:39.620
So I was hoping that the speakers could comment
on sort of what they see about happening in

00:57:39.620 --> 00:57:47.650
both of these societies as well as the state
actors and the organizations that we've been

00:57:47.650 --> 00:57:48.650
talking about.

00:57:48.650 --> 00:57:49.650
Thank you.

00:57:49.650 --> 00:57:52.460
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Steve, why don't you take a crack
at that, because you've got recent on-the-ground

00:57:52.460 --> 00:57:54.600
experience there, and I want to get to some
other questions.

00:57:54.600 --> 00:57:58.080
&gt;&gt;COLL: Well, just to speak of Pakistan, Pakistan
is a very young society.

00:57:58.080 --> 00:58:03.000
It's also a very plugged-in society, and it
has a lot of the demographic and connectivity

00:58:03.000 --> 00:58:06.050
characteristics that preceded the Arab Spring.

00:58:06.050 --> 00:58:12.730
I think the rise of confidence in civil society
and the media and the judiciary reflects a

00:58:12.730 --> 00:58:16.850
sense that Pakistan is changing from the bottom
up.

00:58:16.850 --> 00:58:21.980
And I think the army recognizes that, and
that's why they would, I think, be unwise

00:58:21.980 --> 00:58:25.830
and recognize they would be unwise to intervene
in politics the way they have in the past,

00:58:25.830 --> 00:58:28.710
as Dan was pointing out earlier.

00:58:28.710 --> 00:58:32.850
They're -- the polling -- one -- somebody
mentioned before about the role the United

00:58:32.850 --> 00:58:35.650
States now plays in the life of Pakistan.

00:58:35.650 --> 00:58:40.900
We did a -- some polling recently, where it
was sort of startling to realize that many

00:58:40.900 --> 00:58:45.680
more Pakistanis regard the United States as
an enemy than regard India as an enemy.

00:58:45.680 --> 00:58:51.930
Considering that at the time that we did that
polling, India had recently concluded what

00:58:51.930 --> 00:58:59.180
you might call its fourth war with Pakistan
in 1999, and we were providing substantial

00:58:59.180 --> 00:59:08.140
aid to the country, the sense of being under
siege, I think, is very widely felt in the

00:59:08.140 --> 00:59:09.140
country.

00:59:09.140 --> 00:59:10.750
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Well, we're going to have to wrap
this discussion up.

00:59:10.750 --> 00:59:13.870
I got time for one more quick question.

00:59:13.870 --> 00:59:16.340
I see Joe back there.

00:59:16.340 --> 00:59:21.190
And then you can grab us afterwards or something,
but quick question from Joe Cirincione..

00:59:21.190 --> 00:59:22.282
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Thank you.

00:59:22.282 --> 00:59:23.990
Thank you very much for comments.

00:59:23.990 --> 00:59:25.920
Joe Cirincione, Ploughshares Fund.

00:59:25.920 --> 00:59:30.590
And thank you, Tom (sp), for your fluid chairing
of this insightful panel.

00:59:30.590 --> 00:59:35.100
Next week I'm going to a meeting where I'm
expected to give recommendations for U.S.

00:59:35.100 --> 00:59:38.920
policy towards Pakistan, and I have no idea
what to say.

00:59:38.920 --> 00:59:39.920
(Laughter.)

00:59:39.920 --> 00:59:41.420
So help me out.

00:59:41.420 --> 00:59:47.322
Give me the one thing, each of you, that the
U.S. should be doing with Pakistan that it's

00:59:47.322 --> 00:59:48.450
not doing now.

00:59:48.450 --> 00:59:50.610
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: One sentence each.

00:59:50.610 --> 00:59:53.430
Dan, you're a policy planning guy, so you
can start.

00:59:53.430 --> 00:59:54.430
&gt;&gt;MARKEY: Sure.

00:59:54.430 --> 00:59:57.940
I'll go -- I will -- I will take the safe
route -- go back to a recommendation that

00:59:57.940 --> 01:00:04.490
was in our task force report, which has been
made for about a decade now and still hasn't

01:00:04.490 --> 01:00:08.940
been acted upon, and it has to do with our
trading relationship with Pakistan.

01:00:08.940 --> 01:00:14.010
There's a -- there's an opportunity there
in the textile sector to open up.

01:00:14.010 --> 01:00:19.960
Now, this is politically dead on Capitol Hill
right now because nobody is particularly eager

01:00:19.960 --> 01:00:21.990
to do more for Pakistan.

01:00:21.990 --> 01:00:26.420
But once we hopefully get back to a position
where we recognize that Pakistan's -- excuse

01:00:26.420 --> 01:00:31.420
me -- longer-term stability is of a meaningful
significance to us, and we start to ask how

01:00:31.420 --> 01:00:37.270
we might do that without spending billions
-- tens of billions of dollars in aid, trade

01:00:37.270 --> 01:00:39.430
starts to look more palatable.

01:00:39.430 --> 01:00:40.490
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Bob?

01:00:40.490 --> 01:00:46.700
&gt;&gt;GRENIER: I guess my first piece of advice
would be figure out what the U.S. posture

01:00:46.700 --> 01:00:53.100
in Afghanistan post-2014 is going to look
like, and then get there as quickly as you

01:00:53.100 --> 01:01:02.190
can and as credibly as you can, because unless
we can communicate that in a credible way,

01:01:02.190 --> 01:01:07.420
we won't have the basis on which to reach
a whole series of other agreements with the

01:01:07.420 --> 01:01:08.840
-- with the Pakistanis.

01:01:08.840 --> 01:01:13.850
And a whole series of decisions on the part
of other players will be predicated -- (from

01:01:13.850 --> 01:01:18.270
?) the part of the Pakistanis, the part of
the Afghan Taliban and others -- on what the

01:01:18.270 --> 01:01:23.260
continuing U.S. posture in Afghanistan for
the long term is -- (inaudible) -- going to

01:01:23.260 --> 01:01:24.260
look like.

01:01:24.260 --> 01:01:31.190
&gt;&gt;COLL: Yeah, I think the record of failure
of U.S. policy in Pakistan is informed, you

01:01:31.190 --> 01:01:40.550
know, by this pattern of constantly privileging
short-term security crises over long-term

01:01:40.550 --> 01:01:46.380
civilian control over the country and the
development of normal civil-military relations

01:01:46.380 --> 01:01:53.280
in Pakistan, as well as a pattern of coming
and going in emotional states every 10 years.

01:01:53.280 --> 01:02:03.380
And so I would wish to reset, since resetting
is now necessary, around a long -- a clear,

01:02:03.380 --> 01:02:11.410
durable, long-term commitment to civilian-
led democracy in Pakistan as articulated by

01:02:11.410 --> 01:02:12.870
Pakistanis themselves.

01:02:12.870 --> 01:02:15.290
That is what Pakistan wishes for itself.

01:02:15.290 --> 01:02:20.790
It has a constitutional system that has often
produced very flawed civilian leaders.

01:02:20.790 --> 01:02:25.440
That policy does not require the diminishment
of the army's role in protecting Pakistan's

01:02:25.440 --> 01:02:30.430
borders and national security, but it does
mean returning to the first principles of

01:02:30.430 --> 01:02:38.540
American relations with big, large quasi-democracies
like Pakistan, which to have the courage of

01:02:38.540 --> 01:02:40.100
our own convictions.

01:02:40.100 --> 01:02:46.800
Even at every moment that we're tested, we
are often -- we often mute ourselves in the

01:02:46.800 --> 01:02:53.940
context of Pakistan in ways we would never
would in Indonesia or Tunisia or even Colombia

01:02:53.940 --> 01:02:56.470
or Mexico.

01:02:56.470 --> 01:03:01.530
And so I think that we've got to stop deterring
ourselves from the pursuit of this -- of the

01:03:01.530 --> 01:03:05.330
Pakistan that so many Pakistanis themselves
envision.

01:03:05.330 --> 01:03:08.190
&gt;&gt;IGNATIUS: Well, Joe, I get -- you've got
some talking points now.

01:03:08.190 --> 01:03:11.130
And I hope that -- (laughter) -- I hope that's
helpful.

01:03:11.130 --> 01:03:15.190
Well, I'd like to thank Steve Coll and Bob
Grenier and Dan Markey for coming.

01:03:15.190 --> 01:03:17.600
I'd like to thank you all.

01:03:17.600 --> 01:03:18.600
(Applause.)

01:03:18.600 --> 01:03:23.690
And let me point out that if a veteran reporter
like Pam Constable was confused about whether

01:03:23.690 --> 01:03:39.430
we were on the record, we should reaffirm
this was on the record.

01:03:39.430 --> 01:03:43.790
Thanks.

